# Valkey 8.1.10 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# Valkey is NOT configured by environment variables. It is configured by
# valkey.conf and by command-line flags, and this template uses flags: the
# compose's `command:` runs
#   valkey-server --requirepass ${VALKEY_PASSWORD} --appendonly yes
# Everything else you might want to change (maxmemory, maxmemory-policy,
# appendfsync, save, tls-*, aclfile, bind) is a flag or a conf directive, not
# a variable. This file is therefore short on purpose.
#
# Source: `grep -rn 'getenv(' src/` at tag 8.1.10, plus the official image's
# Dockerfile and docker-entrypoint.sh (valkey-io/valkey-container, 8.1/alpine),
# plus behaviour verified by running valkey/valkey:8.1.10-alpine locally
# (digest sha256:cfb2aa4c8352930130fd45eb231a57310ac326d7323edae12b384b9270c46dda).
# `valkey-server` calls getenv() exactly twice in the whole binary, for
# UPSTART_JOB and NOTIFY_SOCKET (the `supervised auto` probe, meaningless
# under Docker). It reads no configuration variable of any kind.
#
# There are NO `VALKEY_*` settings variables in the official image. The
# separate, unrelated bitnami/valkey image invents VALKEY_PASSWORD,
# VALKEY_AOF_ENABLED, VALKEY_ALLOW_REMOTE_CONNECTIONS and friends; none of
# those exist here and setting them does nothing. VALKEY_PASSWORD below is
# otterdeploy's own prompt: docker compose interpolates it into the
# `--requirepass` flag, and the container never sees a variable by that name.
# The image does bake `ENV VALKEY_VERSION=8.1.10`, which is informational
# (nothing reads it), so it is not declared here.
#
# AUTH POSTURE: this instance IS password-protected, but only because
# `--requirepass` is passed. Two things are worth knowing. (1) The official
# image patches `protected-mode` to default OFF (a sed on src/config.c in the
# Dockerfile, because Docker does not auto-publish ports), so protected-mode is
# not a second line of defence here (`CONFIG GET protected-mode` returns "no").
# (2) An empty VALKEY_PASSWORD does not fail closed: `--requirepass ""` starts
# a server whose default user is `on nopass ~* &* +@all`, i.e. anyone who can
# reach port 6379 gets every key, FLUSHALL, and REPLICAOF (point the instance
# at an attacker's primary and it discards its own dataset). The classic
# Redis-lineage "CONFIG SET dir + dbfilename to write an SSH key" escalation to
# RCE is blocked at this version: `dir` and `dbfilename` are protected configs
# and `enable-protected-configs`/`enable-debug-command`/`enable-module-command`
# all default to "no" (verified), but the scripting engine has repeatedly been
# an RCE surface (CVE-2024-31449, CVE-2025-49844, CVE-2026-56684,
# CVE-2026-63639), so an unauthenticated instance should be treated as fully
# compromised. Always let the platform generate the password. This template
# defines no ACL users beyond the built-in `default`.
#
# CVE STATUS at 8.1.10: no open advisories. It carries the 2026-08-31 security
# release (GHSA-jcj7-v34w-v9vv, an RDMA use-after-free unreachable here in
# any case, since this image is built without USE_RDMA). The unauthenticated
# Lua-interpreter use-after-free disclosed the same day, GHSA-fq2f-crmw-q97r,
# is 9.x-only: it lives in the Lua module introduced in 9.0, and the 8.x
# non-module script debugger is not affected.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("valkey.conf: the real configuration surface", https://valkey.io/topics/valkey.conf/)
# ---

# ─── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The server password. Not read by the container as a variable: docker compose
# substitutes it into the `--requirepass` flag on the `command:` line, which
# also means it is visible in `docker inspect` and in the container's own
# /proc/<pid>/cmdline. Leaving it empty produces `--requirepass ""`, which
# silently disables authentication entirely; see the header. Generate it
# (`openssl rand -base64 32`); never type one.
# @required
# @sensitive
# @docs("Valkey security", https://valkey.io/topics/security/)
VALKEY_PASSWORD=

# Set by the compose to the same value as VALKEY_PASSWORD. This is what makes
# the healthcheck work: valkey-cli is still Redis-named internally (the
# constant is literally CLI_AUTH_ENV "REDISCLI_AUTH" in src/valkey-cli.c at
# 8.1.10), and without it `valkey-cli ping` answers "NOAUTH Authentication
# required." against a requirepass server, so the healthcheck's `grep -q PONG`
# never matches and the container never reports healthy. The server itself
# ignores this variable; only the CLI reads it, so it costs nothing but also
# protects nothing. It does mean any shell in the container is pre-authorised.
# @sensitive
# @docs("valkey-cli", https://valkey.io/topics/cli/)
REDISCLI_AUTH=${VALKEY_PASSWORD}

# ─── Image entrypoint ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The one variable the official image itself consumes. docker-entrypoint.sh
# ends in `exec "$@" $VALKEY_EXTRA_FLAGS`, so whatever you put here is appended
# (unquoted, word-split) to the compose's `command:`. This is the supported
# way to add a valkey-server flag without editing the template, e.g.
# `--maxmemory 512mb --maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru`. Verified to take effect
# at this tag. Empty means no extra flags. Do not put the password here: later
# flags win, so a stray `--requirepass` here would override the compose's.
# @docs("Official valkey container image", https://github.com/valkey-io/valkey-container)
VALKEY_EXTRA_FLAGS=

# ─── Misc ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Standard libc timezone, honored because the image installs tzdata. Valkey
# does not read it directly; it changes the wall-clock in the server's log line
# timestamps (verified: TZ=Asia/Tokyo shifts them). Empty means UTC. It has no
# effect on stored data or key expiry, which are absolute.
TZ=
